{{!-- The numbers, in one line: the COLLAPSED density of the top band.

      One component, two densities — the steading's own device. This is not a second, compact copy
      of the numbers; it is the same numbers at line height, and the fold handle picks between them.
      Nothing runs to make that true: both densities are in the markup at every width, and this one
      stands down whole while the band is on screen.

      The band's numbers only — the stats and HP/Armor/Damage. The rail's XP and Level used to hand
      off here too, and it was the wrong trade: a shut rail with an open band left this line holding
      a 27px row for two numbers under the whole band, which is furniture, not a reminder. XP and
      Level live in the rail and nowhere else now, so putting the rail away puts them away.

      Where each number CAME FROM travels with it. At full density that sentence is a hover on the
      frame's label and a short note under the frame; here the hover comes too, and the sentence
      itself rides along clipped — Foundry's tooltips bind pointer events only and are invisible to
      assistive tech, so a hover can never be the only carrier.

      Plain readouts, not tiles. At line height there is no size at which the woodcut frame is a
      frame rather than a smudge, and a stat's value is the only part of its tile a folded line has
      room for — the name, the note and the debility's effect are the full density's business.

      Readouts, with two exceptions that the fold itself argues for: every stat and the damage die
      ROLL from here, and HP can be stepped here. The reader folds this band precisely to get the
      numbers and the moves on screen together, so anything they would otherwise have to unfold to do
      is the fold costing them the thing it was for. Everything else is a readout and the fold is one
      click from the fields. A stat you cannot roll from the line makes that fold cost the thing it was for. The
      name IS the button, exactly as it is at full density and on the steading's own line, so there is
      one control and nothing to keep in step. It does NOT borrow the tile's `.stonetop-stat-roll`
      class to get there: that one is absolutely positioned, a chip punched into the frame's top rule,
      and wearing it on a line took all six abbreviations out of flow and left the values as six bare
      zeroes. `.rollable[data-roll]` is the whole of what the sheet's delegation needs. Every value is still named for assistive tech, so
      nothing here is a bare number.

      Context: the root. --}}
<div class="stonetop-folded-ledger" aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.foldedLedger'}}">

	<ul class="stonetop-folded-stats stonetop-unmarked">
		{{#each stonetop.statPairs}}
		{{#each stats}}
		<li class="stonetop-folded-stat{{#if ../debility.active}} is-hindered{{/if}}">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-folded-abbr rollable" data-roll="{{key}}"
			        aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.rollStatNamed' stat=name}}"
			        data-tooltip="{{description}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{abbr}}</button>
			<span class="stonetop-folded-value">{{value}}</span>
			{{!-- The hindered ones say why, rather than only looking different: colour is never the
			      only carrier of anything. --}}
			<span class="stonetop-folded-sr">{{#if ../debility.active}}{{../debility.name}}{{/if}}</span>
		</li>
		{{/each}}
		{{/each}}
	</ul>

	<ul class="stonetop-folded-vitals stonetop-unmarked">
		{{!-- HP is the exception to "no second editor", and the reason is the fold itself: the band is
		      folded precisely so the numbers and the moves are on screen together, and HP is the one
		      number that changes while you are looking at the moves. Folding to get at your moves and
		      then unfolding to spend a hit point is the fold costing you the thing it was for.

		      Two carets, not a second field to type in. They are the steading's own line stepper — out
		      of flow, flanking the number, revealed on hover or focus — so they cost the line nothing
		      at rest and the value is still a readout you can see at a glance. They flank the PAIR,
		      "16/16", rather than the editable half of it: sitting against the field alone, ▴ was drawn
		      over the maximum beside it. The input writes through
		      the same `hp` change action the framed field does; there is one handler, and the other
		      density is not on screen to disagree with it. --}}
		<li class="stonetop-folded-hp"><span class="stonetop-folded-abbr"
			data-tooltip="{{stonetop.vitals.sources.hp}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">HP</span><span class="stonetop-folded-sr">{{stonetop.vitals.sources.hp}}</span> <span class="stonetop-folded-value"><span class="stonetop-stepper"><input
			class="stonetop-folded-input stonetop-step" type="number" data-change-action="hp"
			aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.hpCurrent'}}"
			value="{{stonetop.vitals.hp.value}}" min="0">/{{stonetop.vitals.hp.max}}{{> "stonetop.stepper-buttons"}}</span></span></li>
		<li><span class="stonetop-folded-abbr"
			data-tooltip="{{stonetop.vitals.sources.armor}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{localize "stonetop.character.attributes.armor"}}</span><span class="stonetop-folded-sr">{{stonetop.vitals.sources.armor}}</span> <span class="stonetop-folded-value">{{stonetop.vitals.armor}}</span></li>
		<li><button type="button" class="stonetop-folded-abbr rollable" data-roll="damage"
			        data-tooltip="{{stonetop.vitals.sources.damage}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{localize "stonetop.character.attributes.damage"}}</button> <span class="stonetop-folded-sr">{{stonetop.vitals.sources.damage}}</span> <span class="stonetop-folded-value">{{stonetop.vitals.damage.value}}</span></li>
	</ul>

</div>
